15. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The implementation is acceptable only when all applicable criteria below are met.

A. One business dispatch policy

Notification Engine, Marketing Engine, Admin Composer, and all other business SMS/WhatsApp sources use the same authoritative Cloud Run dispatcher.

B. Consistent provider ordering

SENT.dm is primary, followed by eligible Twilio and Termii fallbacks according to the defined failure and eligibility policy.

C. No independent frontend provider dispatch

Frontend components cannot bypass the authoritative dispatcher by calling SENT.dm, Twilio, or Termii directly.

D. No competing Edge Function dispatch policy

Supabase Edge Functions may orchestrate events or act as controlled adapters, but they cannot independently implement a conflicting business provider-selection policy.

E. Authentication remains Supabase-first

The real authentication delivery mechanism follows the intended provider policy without bypassing Supabase authentication controls.

F. Duplicate protection

Idempotency, concurrent-dispatch protection, callback handling, and uncertain-outcome reconciliation are implemented appropriately.

G. Unified durable records

Logical messages and provider attempts are traceable in Supabase without creating unnecessary duplicate ledgers.

H. Business-specific behaviour remains intact

Marketing attribution, notification event correlation, admin conversation context, and relevant consent and permission rules are preserved.

I. No unrelated regressions

Email, push, voice, payments, bookings, and other unrelated workflows remain intact.

J. Evidence-based completion

The report identifies exactly what was changed, what was tested, what passed, what failed, and what still requires deployment or external configuration.

